menu "Telegram bot transport"

    config TELEGRAM_BOT_CERT_BUNDLE
        bool "Validate api.telegram.org against the ESP-IDF certificate bundle"
        default n
        select MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE
        help
            When enabled, the TLS connection to api.telegram.org is validated
            against the certificate bundle shipped with ESP-IDF, which keeps
            working when Telegram rotates its certificate chain.

            It is also what covers a host answered by several front-ends,
            each presenting a chain rooted at a different authority.

            When disabled, the trusted roots are read at run time from a PEM
            file stored in the LittleFS storage partition. That file can be
            replaced from the web admin's File Storage page whenever the
            server chain changes, without rebuilding the firmware, but it
            only trusts the authorities it actually carries.

    config TELEGRAM_BOT_CERT_PATH
        string "Path of the root certificate file"
        depends on !TELEGRAM_BOT_CERT_BUNDLE
        default "/storage/telegram_certificate.pem"
        help
            Absolute path of the PEM file holding the trusted roots of
            api.telegram.org. It must sit inside STORAGE_BASE_PATH, the
            mount point of the LittleFS partition brought up by
            main/storage.c, and it is read once, when telegram_bot_init()
            runs, so the file system has to be mounted first.

            Several certificates may be concatenated in the file and each
            becomes a trust anchor. api.telegram.org is answered by more
            than one front-end and they do not all chain to the same
            authority, so a file carrying a single root validates only the
            connections that land on a matching front-end and fails the
            others with mbedTLS error -0x2700. The count actually loaded is
            logged at start-up.

    config TELEGRAM_BOT_CERT_MAX_LEN
        int "Largest accepted certificate file"
        depends on !TELEGRAM_BOT_CERT_BUNDLE
        range 512 16384
        default 8192
        help
            Size in bytes above which the certificate file is rejected
            instead of being loaded. It bounds the heap the transport
            holds for the whole life of the connection, and therefore how
            many roots the file may concatenate: a PEM root runs to roughly
            1.5 kB, so the default leaves room for a handful.

endmenu
